# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Symbulator — built-in examples
#
# This file fills the "Example" dropdown on the site. Edit it freely:
# add, remove or reorder circuits and reload the page (no restart
# needed). If this file is missing or empty the site still works, just
# without examples.
#
# FORMAT
#   [Name]            starts an entry; the name is the dropdown label
#   anything else     a circuit line, exactly as typed in the textarea
#   key: value        optional setup (see below)
#   # ...             a comment
#
# Nothing about *parsing* cares what order lines come in within a
# entry -- a "key: value" line is always metadata and anything else is
# always a circuit line, wherever it falls. "Save inputs to new entry"
# (on the site itself) writes the circuit lines first and
# then the keys below, grouped analysis-type-and-Expert-Mode, then
# Settings, then Evaluate/Solve-equations/Plot -- the examples here
# follow that same layout for consistency, but you don't have to.
#
# SETTINGS
#   analysis:   dc | ac | fd | tr          (default dc)
#   omega:      angular frequency, for ac  (e.g. 1000, or a symbol)
#   variables:  which answers to transform, for tr (e.g. v_2, i_l1)
#   tool:       th | er | port          (leave out for a normal solve)
#   n1:, n2:    the two port nodes, for th/er/port
#   kind:       z | y | h | g | a | b      (for tool: port)
#   equation:   an expert-mode added equation (repeat for more)
#   condition:  an expert-mode substitution (repeat for more)
#   unknowns:   extra symbols to solve for, comma-separated
#   note:       a line of your own about this entry, shown when it is
#               loaded. Nothing in the app writes one -- add it by hand
#               here -- but it survives being loaded and saved again.
#   rounding:   exact | approx | a number of significant digits (default exact)
#   si:         yes | no -- SI-prefix display, e.g. 1.2 k instead of 1200 (default no)
#   units:      yes | no -- show V/A/W/Ω units on answers (default yes)
#   rms:        yes | no -- RMS phasor convention for AC power (default no).
#               Only written for AC circuits; it does nothing elsewhere.
#   evaluate:   an expression to evaluate against the solved answers
#   solve_equation:   an equation for the standalone equation solver (repeat for more)
#   solve_condition:  a substitution for it (repeat for more)
#   solve_unknowns:   which symbols it should solve for, comma-separated
#   solve_real_only:  yes | no -- keep only its real solutions (default no)
#
# PLOT
#   Plotting is its own card in the app, separate from the Analysis
#   tool above -- the Tool dropdown offers only th/er/port -- so it has
#   its own key and a circuit can carry both. Older files put the plot
#   type in "tool:" instead; that is still read, but new saves use
#   plottool.
#
#   plottool:   plot_time | bode
#   plotkey:    the variable to plot
#   plotmin, plotmax:  time range in s, or frequency range in Hz for
#               a bode plot
#   plotpoints: how many samples to take (default 300)
#
# Every one of these is optional -- leave a key out and the app's own
# default applies, exactly as if it had never been touched.
#
# TIP: write 1'k for "one kilo-ohm" and 1*k for "one times variable k".
# A bare 1k is ambiguous and the site will stop to ask which you meant.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------

title: A sample of what Symbulator can do

[Voltage divider (DC)]

e1,1,0,5
r1,1,2,1'k
r2,2,0,1'k

analysis: dc


[Resistive ladder (DC)]

e1,1,0,36
r1,1,2,1'k
r2,2,3,3'k
r3,3,0,2'k

analysis: dc


[Series RLC (AC, w=1000)]

e1,1,0,10
r1,1,2,100
l1,2,3,0.1
c1,3,0,1e-6

analysis: ac
omega: 1000


[RC step response (TR)]

e1,1,0,5
r1,1,2,1000
c1,2,0,1e-6

analysis: tr
variables: v_2


[RL discharge, I0 = 3A (TR)]

l1,0,2,0.2,3
r1,2,0,100

analysis: tr
variables: i_l1


[Fully symbolic divider (DC)]

e1,1,0,vin
r1,1,2,r_a
r2,2,0,r_b

analysis: dc


[Inverting op-amp, gain -4 (DC)]

e1,1,0,2
rin,1,2,1'k
rf,2,3,4'k
o1,0,2,3

analysis: dc


[Thevenin equivalent at node 2 (tool)]

e1,1,0,12
r1,1,2,4'k
r2,2,0,2'k

tool: th
n1: 2
n2: 0
analysis: dc


[Two-port z parameters of a T network (tool)]

r1,1,3,100
r2,2,3,200
r3,3,0,50

tool: port
kind: z
n1: 1
n2: 2
analysis: dc


[Design: find r_b so that v2 = 6 V (expert)]

e1,1,0,12
r1,1,2,4'k
r2,2,0,r_b

analysis: dc
equations: v_2 = 6
unknowns: r_b


[RC step response, plotted (plot vs. time)]

e1,1,0,5
r1,1,2,1'k
c1,2,0,1'u

analysis: tr
plottool: plot_time
plotkey: v_2
plotmin: 0
plotmax: 0.005
plotpoints: 200


[RC low-pass, Bode plot]

e1,1,0,1
r1,1,2,1'k
c1,2,0,1'u

analysis: fd
plottool: bode
plotkey: v_2
plotmin: 10
plotmax: 100000
plotpoints: 200
